SnarkMux: Streaming SNARK Aggregation
with Constant Online Verification
No Author Given
No Institute Given
Abstract. SNARKs let a verifier check an expensive computation via a
succinct proof, without re-executing the computation. However, many
applications still verify proofs individually, creating verifier load and
high fees in replicated or constrained-verifier settings. Existing proof
aggregation schemes largely follow the generalized inner product argu-
ment (GIPA) line, achieving O(log n) proof size and verifier work for
aggregating n proofs, but remaining costly in practice and operating in
batch mode. Rather than batching until a full window is available, we
presentSnarkMux,astreamingSNARKaggregationschemethatmain-
tains a constant-size aggregate and requires O(1) online verifier work
per window of size n. Motivated by incremental verifiable computation,
SnarkMux directly folds the Groth16 and Plonk verification relations
and composes these proof-system-specific folds with Nova’s circuit-layer
fold; this defers expensive target-verifier logic (e.g., pairings) to the final
checkwhilekeepingconstantaggregatorstate.Aspartofpublic-inputag-
gregation, we propose a generic instance-delegation technique: the prover
aggregates instances, and the verifier performs a simple binding prepro-
cessing costing only O(n) hashes per window, after which online verifi-
cation is constant-cost. Notably, our Groth16 and Plonk instantiations
coverbothcircuit-specificanduniversal-setupSNARKs,withpreprocess-
ing that makes Plonk challenges folding-compatible, leading to a 0.8 KB
Groth16 aggregate with 18 ms verification and a 0.5 KB Plonk one with
2 ms verification for 256 proofs.
Keywords: Proof Aggregation· Incrementally Verifiable Computation
· Folding Scheme.
1 Introduction
Succinct non-interactive argumentof knowledge (SNARK)lets aproverconvince
a verifier that a statement is true using a short proof and efficient verification,
without interaction. A zk-SNARK additionally provides zero-knowledge, mean-
ing the proof reveals nothing beyond validity. SNARK verification is increas-
inglydeployedinsecurity-criticalsystemswithconstrainedorreplicatedverifiers,
where verification cost directly limits throughput and usability. In blockchains,
zk-rollups submit proofs to a layer-1 chain to attest to off-chain state tran-
sitions [26], and zkEVM systems prove correct smart-contract execution [21].
Despite this promise, deployed SNARK-based systems face practical resource

